Up to this point, the discussion has focused on the absorption and transmitted flux along a single sightline, labelled in the straight-line approximation by its transverse comoving position \(\vec{x}_{\perp}\). In many applications, however, one is interested not only in an individual transmitted-flux profile, but also in the statistical fluctuations of that quantity across an ensemble of sightlines or realizations. For that reason, it is convenient to introduce a normalized fluctuation field built from the transmission and its mean value. Note that the quantity we compute from the gas, \(T = e^{-	au}\), is the transmission, not the flux. The flux measured by the observer along a given sightline is \(F = F_0\,T\), where \(F_0\) is the unabsorbed continuum of the background source as received by the observer. This continuum is sightline dependent (for quasar spectra it is usually written \(C_q\)), so in this subsection we denote it by \(F_0^{(s)}(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambda)\). Following the convention of observational Ly\(\alpha\)-forest analyses, the fluctuation field is defined as the observed flux divided by its expectation, the continuum times the mean transmission. The continuum then cancels, and the fluctuation field depends only on the transmission. In the remaining of this subsection – for notational purposes, and to avoid crowded expressions – we will simply denote a given line by the index “\(j\)”, dropping the contextual species index “\(i\)”. The fractional flux fluctuation field, or overflux, associated with transition \(j\), for a given realization \(s\), is then defined as  egin{align} \delta_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) &\;=\;  rac{ F_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) }{ F_{0}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) } -1 =  rac{ T_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) }{  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) } -1, 	ag{3.26} \  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) &\coloneqq \left\langle T_{j}^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ightangle_\mathrm{ens} \quad \Longrightarrow \quad T_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) =  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) \left[ 1+ \delta_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ight] . 
otag \end{align} Here \( ar{T}_{j}(\Lambda)\) denotes the mean transmission associated with line \(j\) (in the literature usually written \( ar{F}\) and called the mean transmitted flux fraction). It is a common convention to interpret this quantity as an ensemble average at fixed spectral coordinate (rather than as an average along a single sightline), and is therefore generally regarded as a smooth function of wavelength or, equivalently, of the absorber redshift associated with that wavelength. Consequently, \(\langle \delta_{j}^{(s)} angle_\mathrm{ens} = 0\) at every \(\Lambda\), whereas the mean of \(\delta_{j}^{(s)}\) along a single sightline does not vanish in general. Normalizing each sightline by its own mean transmission would define a different field. If several lines contribute simultaneously, and the set of lines under consideration is \(\{j=1,\dots,L\}\), then the total transmission is:  egin{align} T^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) = \prod_{j=1}^{L}  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) \left[ 1+ \delta_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ight]. 	ag{3.27} \end{align} The total mean transmission can be computed as:  egin{align}  ar{T}\!(\Lambda) \coloneqq & \left\langle T^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ightangle_\mathrm{ens} = \left\langle \prod_{j=1}^{L} T_{j}^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ightangle_\mathrm{ens} = \left\langle \prod_{j=1}^{L}  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) \left[ 1+ \delta_{j}^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ight] ightangle_\mathrm{ens} = 
otag \ & = \prod_{j=1}^{L}  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) \left\langle \prod_{j=1}^{L} 1+\delta_{j}^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ightangle_\mathrm{ens} , 	ag{3.28} \end{align} and the total overflux is given by  egin{align} \delta^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight)+1 \coloneqq  rac{ F^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) }{ F_{0}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ar{T}\!(\Lambda) } =  rac{ T^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) }{  ar{T}\!(\Lambda) } 
otag =  rac{ \displaystyle \prod_{j=1}^{L} \left[ 1+ \delta_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ight] }{ \displaystyle \left\langle \prod_{j=1}^{L} \left[ 1+ \delta_{j}^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ight] ightangle_\mathrm{ens} }. \end{align}

For quick reference, we collect here the main equations of the transmitted-flux calculation, ordered from the final observable quantities down to the local gas properties from which they are constructed. The full construction is:  egin{align*} \lambda'(\vec{x},\Lambda) \,ightarrow\, 
u_j(\vec{x},\Lambda) \,ightarrow\, \phi_{ij}(\vec{x},\Lambda) \,ightarrow\, 	au_{ij}(\vec{x}_{\perp}, \Lambda) \,ightarrow\, T_{ij}(\vec{x}_{\perp}, \Lambda) \,ightarrow\, \delta_{j}(\vec{x}_{\perp}, \Lambda) \end{align*} For a given realization \(s\) and line \(j\) – with the contextual species index \(i\) suppressed – the overflux is:  egin{align} \delta_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) + 1 &\;=\;  rac{ F_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) }{ F_{0}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) } =  rac{ T_{j}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) }{  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) }, \quad \mathrm{with}\quad  ar{T}_{j}\!(\Lambda) \coloneqq \left\langle T_{j}^{(s)}\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) ightangle_{\mathrm{ens}}. 	ag{3.37} \end{align} If several lines contribute simultaneously, the total overflux can be written in terms of the line-by-line overfluxes as:  egin{align} &\delta^{(s)}(\vec{x}_\perp, \Lambda) = rac{1}{1 + \mathcal{C}(\Lambda)}\sum_{p=0}^{L} \mathcal{S}_p^{(s)}(\vec{x}_{\perp},\,\Lambda) 	ag{3.38} \end{align} Where we have defined:  egin{align} &\mathcal{S}_0^{(s)}(\Lambda) \coloneqq -\mathcal{C}(\Lambda), \qquad \mathcal{S}_p^{(s)}(\vec{x}_{\perp},\,\Lambda) \coloneqq \sum_{1\le j_1<\cdots<j_p\le L}\; \prod_{r=1}^{p} \delta_{j_r}^{(s)}\!\left(\vec{x}_\perp,\Lambdaight) \quad 	extrm{(order $p$)}, \qquad 1\le p\le L, 
otag \end{align} and  egin{align} \mathcal{C}(\Lambda) &\coloneqq \sum_{1\le j_1<j_2\le L} C_{j_1 j_2}(\Lambda) + \sum_{1\le j_1<j_2<j_3\le L} C_{j_1 j_2 j_3}(\Lambda) + \cdots + C_{1 2 \cdots L}(\Lambda), 	ag{3.39} \ &\quad\mathrm{where}\quad C_{j_1 \cdots j_n}(\Lambda) \coloneqq \left\langle \delta_{j_1}^{(s)}\cdots\delta_{j_n}^{(s)} ightangle_{\mathrm{ens}}. 
otag \end{align} The transmission is obtained from the optical depth, and the transmitted flux by attenuating the emitted continuum \(F_0(\Lambda)\) by the transmission factor:  egin{align} F_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) &= F_0(\Lambda)\,T_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) , \quad T_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) = \exp\!\left[-	au_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight)ight]. 	ag{3.40} \end{align} In the straight-line approximation, the optical depth along the line of sight can be computed as:  egin{align} 	au_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},\Lambdaight) = \mathcal{C}_j \int_{x_{\parallel,\mathrm{em}}}^{x_{\parallel,\mathrm{f}}}  rac{ n_{i\,\mathrm{com}}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},x'_{\parallel}ight) }{ a\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},x'_{\parallel}ight)^{3} } \; \phi_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},x'_{\parallel},\Lambdaight) \; a\!\left(\vec{x}_{\perp},x'_{\parallel}ight)\, dx'_{\parallel}, \qquad \mathcal{C}_j \equiv  rac{e^{2}f_j}{4\varepsilon_{0}m_e c}. 	ag{3.41} \end{align} The local absorption line profile kernel is described by the Voigt profile:  egin{align} \phi_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight) = V\!\left( 
u_j\!\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight); \sigma_{ij}\!\left(\vec{x}ight), \gamma_j\!\left(\vec{x}ight) ight), 	ag{3.42} \end{align} where the frequency offset is  egin{align} 
u_j\!\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight) = c\left(  rac{1}{\lambda'\!\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight)} -  rac{1}{\lambda_j} ight). 	ag{3.43} \end{align} The Doppler factor is defined as:  egin{align} \Dop(\vec{x}) \coloneqq \sqrt{ rac{1- eta(\vec{x})}{1+ eta(\vec{x})}}, \qquad  eta(\vec{x}) \coloneqq  rac{v_{i\,\parallel}(\vec{x})}{c}. \qquad 	extrm{If } \quad v_{i\,\parallel} \ll c \quad \longrightarrow \quad \Dop^{-1}(\vec{x}) \approx 1+  eta(\vec{x}) 	ag{3.44} \end{align} If \(\Lambda=\lambda_{\mathrm{obs}}\), then  egin{align} \lambda'\!\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight) = \lambda_{\mathrm{obs}}\,  rac{a(\vec{x})}{a_{\mathrm{obs}}}\, \Dop^{-1}(\vec{x}). \qquad \lambda_\mathrm{obs} \in \left[ \lambda_{j,\mathrm{min}} \; , \; \lambda_{j,\mathrm{max}}  rac{a_f}{a_{\mathrm{em}}} ight]. 	ag{3.45} \end{align} If \(\Lambda=v_{\dagger}\), then  egin{align} \lambda'_j\!\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight) = \lambda_j\, \exp\!\left( rac{v_{\dagger}}{c}ight)\,  rac{a(\vec{x})}{a_f}\, \Dop^{-1}(\vec{x}). \qquad v_\dagger \in \left[ 0 \; , \; c\ln\left( rac{a_f}{a_{\mathrm{em}}}ight) ight]. 	ag{3.46} \end{align} If \(\Lambda=x\), then  egin{align} \lambda'\left(\vec{x},\Lambdaight) &\approx \lambda_j\left[ 1+  rac{1}{c} \left(  rac{H(z_{\mathrm{box}})}{1+z_{\mathrm{box}}}(\vec{x}-x_\dagger) + v_{i\,\parallel}(\vec{x}) ight) ight]. 	ag{3.47} \end{align} The Gaussian and Lorentzian widths entering the Voigt profile are  egin{align} \sigma_{ij}(\vec{x}) &=  rac{b_i(\vec{x})}{\sqrt{2}\lambda_j}, \qquad b_i(\vec{x}) = \sqrt{ b_{i\,\mathrm{th}}^{2}(\vec{x}) + b_{i\,\mathrm{turb}}^{2}(\vec{x}) }, \qquad b_{i\,\mathrm{th}}(\vec{x}) = \sqrt{ rac{2k_B T_i(\vec{x})}{m_i}}, 	ag{3.48} \[0.3em] \gamma_j(\vec{x}) &=  rac{A_j + A_{j\,\mathrm{coll}}(\vec{x})}{4\pi}. 	ag{3.49} \end{align}
